<main class="section"><div class="container legal-copy">
<span class="eyebrow">Rummy Score privacy</span><h1 style="font-size:clamp(2.7rem,6vw,4.8rem);margin-top:18px">Privacy Policy</h1><p class="lead">Last updated: August 20, 2026</p>
<h2>Overview</h2><p>Rummy Score can be used entirely without an account — local games and Practice vs AI never leave your device. Creating an account adds cloud backup, cross-device sync, and playing with friends in real time, which means the account, game, and friend data described below is stored on our backend so those features work. We collect only what those features need.</p>
<h2>Account information</h2><p>Creating an account collects your email address and a display name you choose. If you sign up with a password, it is handled and stored (hashed) by our authentication provider, Supabase — we never see or store it in plain text. If you sign in with Google, Google shares your name, email address, and profile photo with us as part of that sign-in. You can also use the app as a guest with no account at all, or continue with a temporary anonymous session (for example, to join a shared game someone invited you to) — an anonymous session has no personal information tied to it beyond a device-generated identifier.</p>
<h2>Game data</h2><p>Rummy Score stores game-related information such as player names, game settings, scores, rounds, and activity history. For local games and Practice vs AI, this stays on your device unless you're signed in, in which case it's also backed up to our backend so you can restore it on another device. Shared games (played live with friends across devices) and event details you create are stored on our backend for as long as the game or event exists, since real-time sync across every participant's device is the point of that feature.</p>
<h2>Friends and account linking</h2><p>Linking accounts with another player lets you search for them by display name or email, or share a friend code. A search matches against other users' display names and email addresses, so choosing to search by email means that email address is checked against our records — and your own display name and email become findable the same way if someone searches for you. You can invite someone who doesn't yet have an account by entering their email address, which we send to our authentication provider to deliver a one-time signup invite; we don't otherwise store or reuse an invited email beyond that.</p>
<h2>Push notifications (mobile)</h2><p>If you enable notifications on the mobile app, we store a device push token so we can notify you about things like game invitations. Sending a notification shares that token with Expo, the push delivery service the app uses — we don't share it for any other purpose.</p>
<h2>Analytics and crash reporting</h2><p>The mobile app uses Firebase Analytics and Firebase Crashlytics (Google) to understand app usage and diagnose crashes. The web app does not use any analytics or crash-reporting service. We aim to avoid sending unnecessary personal information in analytics events or crash reports.</p>
<h2>Third-party services we use</h2><ul><li><strong>Supabase</strong> — hosts our database, authentication, and real-time sync; account, game, friend, and event data described above lives here.</li><li><strong>Google</strong> — if you choose to sign in with Google, and for Firebase Analytics/Crashlytics on mobile.</li><li><strong>Expo</strong> — delivers push notifications on mobile.</li><li><strong>Cloudflare</strong> — hosts the web app.</li></ul>
<h2>Information not required for core scorekeeping</h2><ul><li>Precise location</li><li>Contacts</li><li>Real-money payment information</li></ul>
<h2>Deleting your account and data</h2><p>You can permanently delete your account at any time from Settings &gt; Delete Account in the app. This removes your account and the game/friend/event data associated with it from our backend. It does not retroactively remove anonymous analytics or crash reports already sent before deletion, since those aren't tied to your account.</p>
<h2>Children's privacy</h2><p>Rummy Score is intended as a general scorekeeping utility. It is not designed to collect personal information from children for advertising or profiling.</p>
<h2>Changes to this policy</h2><p>This policy may be updated as Rummy Score features or data practices change. The latest version will always be published on this page.</p>
<h2>Contact</h2><p>Questions about privacy can be sent to <a href="mailto:contact@zomsi.co"><strong>contact@zomsi.co</strong></a>.</p>
<h2>Company website</h2><p>This policy covers only the Rummy Score app. For the Zomsi website and company-level privacy practices, see the <a href="/privacy">Zomsi Privacy Policy</a>.</p>
